WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.800
Data lifetime is central to harvest-now, decrypt-later risk.

00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:05.440
Some data loses sensitivity quickly.

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:08.790
A temporary one-time notification, a short-lived session artifact,

00:00:08.790 --> 00:00:11.680
or a public status message may have little future value.

00:00:11.680 --> 00:00:14.155
Other data remains sensitive for years or decades.

00:00:14.155 --> 00:00:17.915
Health information, legal records, government information, authentication secrets,

00:00:17.915 --> 00:00:22.050
business strategy, intellectual property, research data, regulated records,

00:00:22.050 --> 00:00:26.680
and personal data can retain value long after the original transmission or storage event.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:31.095
Confidentiality shelf life is the period during which unauthorized disclosure would still

00:00:31.095 --> 00:00:31.790
matter.

00:00:31.790 --> 00:00:35.105
A patent strategy may be sensitive until a product is launched or protected.

00:00:35.105 --> 00:00:37.880
Medical records may remain private for a person's lifetime.

00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:41.800
Classified or government-sensitive material may have formal protection periods.

00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:45.515
Legal documents may affect negotiations or litigation years later.

00:00:45.515 --> 00:00:47.075
Business plans, merger information,

00:00:47.075 --> 00:00:50.390
and customer data can create harm long after they were created.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:53.930
Long-term archives deserve special attention because they concentrate older assumptions.

00:00:53.930 --> 00:00:58.850
A backup set may contain encrypted databases, file shares, email exports, keys, logs,

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:01.080
application data, and historical records.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.770
If those backups are copied, retained longer than necessary,

00:01:03.770 --> 00:01:06.680
or stored under cryptographic designs that later become weak,

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:10.470
the exposure can outlive the systems that originally created the data.

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:13.480
Retention choices are therefore part of cryptographic risk management.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:16.280
Short-lived and long-lived data should not receive identical treatment.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:20.040
A risk program can focus attention where future disclosure would still be harmful.

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:22.240
That does not mean ignoring short-lived systems;

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:26.115
it means prioritizing sensitive data with long confidentiality requirements.

00:01:26.115 --> 00:01:29.900
Good planning combines classification, retention review, cryptographic inventory,

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:34.060
and business context so teams can distinguish theoretical concern from risk that deserves

00:01:34.060 --> 00:01:35.220
concrete action.
